-- Vocabulary V6 step by step (IELTS-V6 v1.1.0): five guided steps and one mixed check, in the Lesson 6 format
-- (LEARN FIRST worked example, an explanation for every answer). Auto-marked, so filed as reading tasks; every item is
-- mapped to the vocabulary subskill it practises. Owner instruction 2026-10-28 13:01 (Asia/Bishkek): 'Go next'.
-- Named-student pilot with requires_review; nothing is released. Source: docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json.
do $steps$
declare art constant text := '

- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

THE PASSAGE: The fifteen-minute city

A. In recent years, urban planners in a number of European and North American cities have promoted the idea of the ''fifteen-minute city'', a model in which residents can reach most of what they need in daily life, including work, schools, shops, health services and green space, within a quarter of an hour on foot or by bicycle. Rather than concentrating offices in a central business district and housing on the periphery, the model encourages a mixture of uses within each neighbourhood, so that fewer journeys require a car.

B. Advocates present the idea as a response to several problems at once. Shorter journeys, they argue, would curb carbon emissions from transport and alleviate traffic congestion, although even enthusiastic supporters concede that congestion is unlikely to disappear altogether, since deliveries, emergency services and longer trips would still depend on roads. The model is also promoted as a means of improving public health: if walking or cycling becomes the most convenient way to reach a school or a pharmacy, physical activity is built into daily routines rather than reserved for leisure time, which may help to counter the effects of an increasingly sedentary lifestyle.

C. A further claim concerns social life. Supporters suggest that when residents spend more time in their own neighbourhood, they are more likely to recognise their neighbours, use local businesses and take part in community activities. Evidence for this effect is, however, largely drawn from surveys of residents'' own perceptions, and researchers caution that it is difficult to separate the influence of urban design from that of income, age and length of residence.

D. Critics raise a number of objections. The most frequently cited is that the model is far easier to apply in dense, historic city centres than in suburbs built around the car, where homes, workplaces and services are often separated by considerable distances. Converting such areas would require substantial investment and, in many cases, changes to planning regulations that have shaped development for decades. Others point out that many jobs cannot be relocated to residential districts, so that a fifteen-minute radius may cover shopping and schooling but not employment.

E. There are also concerns about fairness. If a neighbourhood becomes more attractive as a result of new parks, cycle lanes and local services, property prices and rents may rise, potentially forcing out the lower-income residents the improvements were intended to benefit. Some housing campaigners therefore argue that any such scheme should be accompanied by measures to protect affordable housing, such as limits on rent increases or a requirement that new developments include a proportion of low-cost homes.

F. Finally, the concept has attracted opposition from a small number of people who interpret it as an attempt to restrict where residents are allowed to travel. Planners involved in such schemes generally reject this interpretation, stressing that the aim is to make local trips more convenient, not to prevent longer ones. Whatever its eventual impact, the debate illustrates how decisions about the layout of a city can have far-reaching consequences for health, the environment and social equality.';
begin
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r88-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 1 of 6: Meet the collocations','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the meaning of each collocation.','A collocation is learned as one piece: curb emissions, a sedentary lifestyle, precarious employment, alleviate congestion.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "What does ''curb carbon emissions'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  reduce the gases that cause global warming"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  build more power stations"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  increase energy production"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  measure the gases in the air"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "What is ''a sedentary lifestyle''?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a life spent travelling"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  a way of life with little physical activity"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a busy social life"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a healthy, active way of life"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "What is ''precarious employment''?", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  work you do as a volunteer"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  well-paid work in an office"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  work with no security, such as short contracts"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  work that is dangerous to health"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "What does ''alleviate traffic congestion'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  close roads to traffic"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  make heavy traffic less severe"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  measure traffic"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  cause more traffic"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "What does ''exacerbate'' a problem mean?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make it less serious"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  explain it"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  solve it completely"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  make it worse"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "What does ''render something obsolete'' mean?", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make it more expensive"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  sell it"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  repair it"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  make it no longer useful"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 6, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, ''Curb'' = limit or reduce: curb carbon emissions.\n2 B, ''Sedentary'' = sitting a lot and moving little.\n3 C, ''Precarious'' = not secure: short contracts and no guaranteed hours.\n4 B, ''Alleviate'' = make a problem less severe.\n5 D, ''Exacerbate'' = make worse. Its opposite is ''mitigate''.\n6 D, ''Render obsolete'' = make no longer useful: streaming has rendered DVDs largely obsolete.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Collocation: learn the meaning of precise topic collocations for common IELTS themes."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: topic words that go together

For common IELTS topics, learn words in pairs and groups, not one at a time:
- curb carbon emissions = reduce the gases that cause global warming
- a sedentary lifestyle = a way of life with little physical activity
- precarious employment = work with no security
- alleviate traffic congestion = make heavy traffic less severe
- exacerbate a problem = make it worse; mitigate a problem = make it less serious
- render something obsolete = make it no longer useful

WORKED EXAMPLE
''An ageing population places a strain on hospitals.''
''Place a strain on'' = create pressure that is difficult to manage.

Now choose the meaning of six collocations.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'68b99919e64be63ee879eef7f67d1930efd04915b551230799714b4ed427f454',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r89-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 2 of 6: Which word completes it?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the word that completes each collocation.','The verb is part of the collocation: strike a balance, raise concerns, place a strain, foster thinking, acquire skills.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Cities are planting more trees to ______ the effects of heatwaves.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  mitigate"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  foster"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  exacerbate"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  render"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Some new apps have ______ privacy concerns among parents.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  grown"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  rose"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  raised"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  lifted"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Universities should ______ critical thinking, not just memory.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  curb"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  foster"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  render"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  alleviate"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Working from home can make it hard to ______ a balance between work and rest.", "accepted_answers": ["D"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  hit"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  do"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  make"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  strike"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "An ageing population ______ a strain on care homes.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  places"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  does"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  gives"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  makes"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Volunteering helps young people ______ transferable skills.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  do"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  acquire"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  reach"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 7, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, mitigate the effects: make them less serious. ''Exacerbate'' would make them worse.\n2 C, raised concerns: ''raise'' takes an object; ''rose'' is the past of ''rise'', which does not.\n3 B, foster critical thinking: encourage it to grow.\n4 D, strike a balance: the fixed collocation.\n5 A, places a strain on: the fixed collocation (''puts'' is also possible).\n6 C, acquire: ''acquire skills'' means gain them through learning or experience.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Use natural collocation: choose the verb that goes with a topic noun."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the verb belongs to the noun

Many collocations are a verb + noun pair. The ''logical'' verb is often wrong:
- STRIKE a balance (not make or do a balance)
- RAISE concerns (not rise or lift concerns)
- PLACE / PUT a strain on (not make or do a strain)
- FOSTER critical thinking
- ACQUIRE transferable skills
- MITIGATE the effects

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Remote work can help people ______ a balance between career and family.''
The fixed verb is ''strike''. -> strike a balance

Now choose six verbs.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'fa0b0ba88f322e04b18b83376cd58969e9ef3435bab803c9a936905ae8a2dd63',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r90-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 3 of 6: Find the collocation in the passage','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then complete each phrase with ONE WORD from the named paragraph. Copy it exactly.','Find the paraphrase in the paragraph and copy the word that completes the collocation.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Paragraph B: ''make heavy traffic less severe'' = ________ traffic congestion", "accepted_answers": ["alleviate"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph B: ''a way of life with little physical activity'' = an increasingly ________ lifestyle", "accepted_answers": ["sedentary"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "Paragraph B: ''reduce the gases from transport'' = ________ carbon emissions from transport", "accepted_answers": ["curb"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Paragraph E: ''homes ordinary people can pay for'' = ________ housing", "accepted_answers": ["affordable"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Paragraph A: ''the outer edge of a city'' = on the ________", "accepted_answers": ["periphery"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Paragraph D: ''a large amount of money'' = substantial ________", "accepted_answers": ["investment"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 alleviate: shorter journeys \"would curb carbon emissions from transport and alleviate traffic congestion\".\n2 sedentary: \"counter the effects of an increasingly sedentary lifestyle\".\n3 curb: \"Shorter journeys, they argue, would curb carbon emissions from transport\".\n4 affordable: \"measures to protect affordable housing\".\n5 periphery: \"housing on the periphery\". The periphery is the outer edge.\n6 investment: \"Converting such areas would require substantial investment\".\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Infer word meaning from context: find topic collocations in a passage from their meaning."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the passage uses the collocations

IELTS passages on common themes use these collocations naturally. Recognising them makes reading faster.
1. Go to the named paragraph.
2. Look for the meaning given.
3. Copy the missing word exactly.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Paragraph F: ''affect many people or areas strongly'' = have ________ consequences.
Proof: "decisions about the layout of a city can have far-reaching consequences for health". -> far-reaching

Now complete six collocations.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'afc95ec49011ef71423b2422f440e2c60bdab63443b3f0ddcd6567fe2e3ad5db',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r91-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 4 of 6: Better or worse?','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose the word or meaning that matches the direction of each change.','Some words make a problem worse (exacerbate), some make it less serious (mitigate, alleviate), some make a thing useless (render obsolete).','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "''Heatwaves can exacerbate air pollution.'' Heatwaves ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make air pollution worse"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  have no effect on air pollution"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  make air pollution less serious"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  remove air pollution"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "''Trees can mitigate the effects of heat.'' Trees ...", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  make the effects of heat less serious"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  make the effects of heat worse"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  measure the heat"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  cause heatwaves"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "What is ''urban sprawl''?", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a new park"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  the uncontrolled spread of a city"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a fast road through a city"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  a crowded city centre"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Rising rents can ______ the housing crisis.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  curb"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  mitigate"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  exacerbate"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  alleviate"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Cheaper public transport could ______ the problem of traffic.", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  exacerbate"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  alleviate"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  raise"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  render"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Smartphones have rendered paper maps largely ______.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "supporting_node_id": "dded4435-86bc-4f98-a822-5796ab2c9540", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  obsoleting"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  obsolescence"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  obsolete"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  obsoletely"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 6, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 A, make it worse: exacerbate means make a problem worse.\n2 A, make the effects less serious: mitigate = reduce the harm.\n3 B, the uncontrolled spread of a city: it often increases commuting times.\n4 C, exacerbate: rising rents make the crisis worse.\n5 B, alleviate: it would make the problem less severe.\n6 C, obsolete: ''render something + adjective''. The adjective is ''obsolete''.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Lexical relationships: distinguish words that make a problem better or worse, and their correct forms."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: worse, better or useless?

These words describe what happens to a problem:
- exacerbate = make WORSE
- mitigate / alleviate = make LESS serious
- curb = limit, stop it growing
- render something obsolete = make it no longer useful
Getting the direction wrong reverses your meaning.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Heatwaves ______ air pollution, while trees can ______ it.''
Heatwaves make pollution worse: exacerbate. Trees make it less serious: mitigate.

Now choose six answers.'),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'59a09a718f34e24b27322080c9d19a430811903a2550242730b57609a936e43a',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r92-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 5 of 6: Same idea, different words','reading','guided_practice','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then choose TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN. The statements use different words from the passage.','The passage uses collocations; the statements paraphrase them. Match the meaning, then check for a clear contradiction.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "In the model, offices are kept in the city centre and homes on the edge.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Supporters admit that roads would still be needed for some journeys.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The evidence about social benefits comes mainly from what residents themselves say.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "Changing suburbs that were built around the car would be cheap.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "Rents have already doubled in neighbourhoods that adopted the model.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Most planners say the model is designed to stop people making longer journeys.", "accepted_answers": ["FALSE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 FALSE. Proof: \"Rather than concentrating offices in a central business district and housing on the periphery, the model encourages a mixture of uses\".\n2 TRUE. Proof: \"deliveries, emergency services and longer trips would still depend on roads\".\n3 TRUE. Proof: \"Evidence for this effect is, however, largely drawn from surveys of residents'' own perceptions\".\n4 FALSE. Proof: \"Converting such areas would require substantial investment\".\n5 NOT GIVEN. The passage says rents \"may rise\", but gives no figures and no examples of this happening.\n6 FALSE. Proof: planners stress \"that the aim is to make local trips more convenient, not to prevent longer ones\".\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "True/False/Not Given with paraphrased topic vocabulary."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: recognise the idea, not the words

Statements will not repeat the passage''s collocations. They paraphrase them:
- ''alleviate traffic congestion'' -> ''reduce traffic problems''
- ''on the periphery'' -> ''on the edge of the city''
- ''substantial investment'' -> ''a lot of money''
Match the meaning first, then decide TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN.

WORKED EXAMPLE
Statement: ''The model aims to put everyday services close to where people live.''
Proof: "residents can reach most of what they need in daily life ... within a quarter of an hour".  -> TRUE

Now check six statements.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'0dd03fe428b61e691f54b9dc695ca8f9f9f799a09b73fe8493b2fd4b0f3c5740',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
 insert into private.ielts_learning_tasks(code,version,title,skill,purpose,instructions,success_description,taxonomy_version_id,pilot_student,questions,content,review_record,content_sha256,requires_review) values('bh-ielts-targeted-reading-r93-v1','0.1.0','Vocabulary V6 step 6 of 6: Mixed check','reading','independent_check','Read LEARN FIRST and the worked example. Then answer six new questions. Work on your own, without notes, a dictionary or help. Tell your teacher if anything interrupted you.','Use the collocation as one piece, check its direction (worse or better), and match paraphrases in the passage.','ad250533-8582-4bc5-986b-32b0c9640a16','b30e9c28-96f1-4d34-83e9-9b28b4926f42','[{"id": "q1", "prompt": "Facial recognition in schools ______ serious privacy concerns.", "accepted_answers": ["C"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  rises"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  grows"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  raises"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  lifts"}]}, {"id": "q2", "prompt": "Paragraph C: Researchers say it is hard to separate the effect of urban ________ from income, age and length of residence.", "accepted_answers": ["design"], "taxonomy_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "supporting_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "answer_format": "text"}, {"id": "q3", "prompt": "The model encourages a mix of homes, shops and services within each area.", "accepted_answers": ["TRUE"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q4", "prompt": "The fifteen-minute city has reduced carbon emissions in every city that has tried it.", "accepted_answers": ["NOT GIVEN"], "taxonomy_node_id": "be5b2be3-ffb1-4cfb-95c8-cb9e3e9101de", "supporting_node_id": "aafe14e8-c993-48b3-ae4a-2c26b2021897", "options": [{"value": "TRUE", "label": "TRUE"}, {"value": "FALSE", "label": "FALSE"}, {"value": "NOT GIVEN", "label": "NOT GIVEN"}]}, {"id": "q5", "prompt": "''Precarious employment'' means ...", "accepted_answers": ["B"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "c90adeab-a6e3-473a-8e53-b86f6738ccbd", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  a well-paid job"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  work with no security"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  a dangerous job"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  working from home"}]}, {"id": "q6", "prompt": "Long commutes can ______ a strain on family life.", "accepted_answers": ["A"], "taxonomy_node_id": "22e5951f-eefd-4db0-9ea8-ce06c4b7127c", "supporting_node_id": "cefbe886-39b2-4efa-9123-6ad04ea0f631", "options": [{"value": "A", "label": "A  place"}, {"value": "B", "label": "B  do"}, {"value": "C", "label": "C  make"}, {"value": "D", "label": "D  raise"}]}]'::jsonb,'{"scope": "Targeted IELTS vocabulary exercise; not a full paper.", "suggested_minutes": 8, "mapping_scope": "Task-specific reviewed focus with canonical English registry references. Not an IELTS/English equivalence or a pooled Academic Profile claim.", "focus_code": "bh-ielts-vocabulary-targeted-focus-v1", "teacher_notes": "1 C, raises: the fixed collocation is ''raise concerns''; ''rise'' cannot take an object.\n2 design: \"it is difficult to separate the influence of urban design from that of income, age and length of residence\".\n3 TRUE. Proof: \"the model encourages a mixture of uses within each neighbourhood\".\n4 NOT GIVEN. Advocates argue shorter journeys \"would curb carbon emissions\", but the passage gives no results from any city.\n5 B, work with no security: short contracts and no guaranteed hours.\n6 A, place a strain on: the fixed collocation.\nRead each explanation, then use the word or phrase in a sentence of your own.", "focus": "Topic collocations: meaning, verb choice, passage paraphrase and True/False/Not Given."}'::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('passage', 'LEARN FIRST: the whole toolkit

- Learn collocations as one piece: raise concerns, place a strain on, strike a balance.
- Check the direction: exacerbate (worse) or mitigate (less serious).
- In a passage, match the meaning, not the words.

WORKED EXAMPLE
''Facial recognition in shops ______ serious privacy concerns.''
The fixed verb with ''concerns'' is ''raise''. -> raises

Now answer six new questions on your own.' || art),'{"author": "Brains Heist AI-assisted original", "rights": "Original Brains Heist text; no third-party passages or questions copied.", "bible": "2.0.0", "human_review": "pending", "difficulty_comparability": "Not calibrated equivalent forms; no bands. Change is judged only by bh-practice-effect-v1 (provisional).", "source": "docs/ielts/practice/TARGETED_VOCABULARY_01.json 0.1.0"}'::jsonb,'9dfc05a5ad67abc3cf49c52d3b693d1ef4887f977160d92c6963a9b5d7961701',true) on conflict (code) do nothing;
end $steps$;
